# Grandfathered findings (ratchet): a (file, kind) pair may
# not exceed its count here. Regenerate after fixing with:
#   python3 tools/audit/check_stm_closures.py --update-baseline kame modules
#
# The gErrPrint entries below are accepted, not merely tolerated, and the reason
# is specific enough to re-check if either half changes (2026-07-29):
#
#   * gErrPrint_redirected takes g_debug_mutex but RELEASES it before returning
#     (kame/support.cpp), so the thread never negotiates while holding it —
#     this is not the mutex-across-negotiation deadlock class.
#   * It then calls XStatusPrinter::printError -> m_tlkTalker.talk(), whose only
#     listener is connected with Listener::FLAG_MAIN_THREAD_CALL
#     (kame/xnodeconnector.cpp:816), so talk() enqueues and returns.  No inline
#     listener, hence no nested transaction on the committing thread.
#
# What remains is a blocking stderr+ofstream flush re-run once per CAS retry, on
# an error path only: duplicated log lines and one syscall.  Not worth a fix.
#
# This is NOT a general licence for gErrPrint inside a closure.  If the mutex
# were ever held across the talk(), or that listener lost
# FLAG_MAIN_THREAD_CALL, these become the hazard the rest of rule 1 is about.
2	kame/driver/primarydriver.cpp	gErrPrint
1	kame/graph/graphntoolbox.cpp	gErrPrint
1	kame/xnodeconnector.cpp	gErrPrint
2	modules/nmr/nmrrelax.cpp	gErrPrint
1	modules/nmr/pulseanalyzer.cpp	gErrPrint
